0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105986 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105986 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105986 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105986 #edb/ref 1045 "Singular Linear Transformations in the Plane" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105986 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186106897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105986 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105986 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "f9fd061e-b62c-59b3-95e7-cb70aa81601f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106897 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106897 #edb/ref 1061 44641 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106897 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "91fe4680-2271-5bd9-a679-84fcae2b774c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106897 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107154 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106898 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106899 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106898 #edb/ref 1061 44642 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106898 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1608691b-a3f7-54f4-82a1-b60b84369109" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106898 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106899 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106899 #edb/ref 1061 44645 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106899 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cdca196d-b54e-5e0f-8148-f89ac24552f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106899 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106900 #edb/ref 1061 44693 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106900 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fe3d3d75-3555-5f42-96ec-0bc13603e728" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106900 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106901 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106901 #edb/ref 1061 44695 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106901 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c766a184-7dd4-51fc-8815-53eaecbc38c4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106901 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107155 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106902 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106900 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106902 #edb/ref 1061 44720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106902 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b9b4bf14-bd02-5562-869e-62d28d1f3854" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106902 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107156 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107154 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the triangle below with vertices $(0,0),$ $(2,1)$ and $(2,3).$\n\n![](images/f7/f7c16deb083b467c063c900196274d08c532ad4633354f375530cb2cbe270491.png)\n\nWhat is the image of this triangle under the action of the transformation  $\\mathbf{T}$ represented by the matrix \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the image of the triangle under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all vertices of our triangle written as columns:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(0,0),$ $(2,0),$ $(2,0).$ Notice that these points lie on the same line. Two of them even coincide!\n\nSo, the image of our triangle under the action of $\\mathbf T$ is the following line segment:\n\n![](images/88/88383b8eca1805c158f80d998d844d4c666b15d118a0ec26b4a1ba43c781fc76.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\nThis is an example of a linear transformation that does not preserve linear independence. \n\n* First, notice that since [math]\\det(T)= 0,[/math] the matrix [math]T[/math] is singular, which means that the vectors represented by the columns of [math] T,[/math] given below, are *not* linearly independent. \n\\[\n\\left\\{ \\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\end{bmatrix}, \\:  \\begin{bmatrix} 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{bmatrix} \\right\\}\n\\]  \n\n- Recall that for any linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ and region $D,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(\\mathbf{T}(D)) = |\\det(T)| \\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\n \nSince $|\\det(T)|  = 0$ in this case, any object applied to $\\mathbf T$ collapses to an object with zero area.\n\n- Also, the fact that $|\\det(T)|  = 0$ tells us that $\\mathbf T$ has no inverse transformation. Therefore, it is impossible to recover the original triangle knowing only its image and the matrix $T.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107154 #edb/ref 1065 7693 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107154 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "260cef8c-3f93-55cc-ac8c-258b2555991e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107154 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107155 #edb/ref 1064 "Given a linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with an associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ there are three possibilities regarding the type of object formed under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ by a two-dimensional preimage. \n\nTo demonstrate, consider the three-sided polygon $\\mathcal P$ with vertices $(0,1)$, $(2,0)$ and $(2,1),$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/97/97689c7aaf7f6d42314fd01cb36b557df8081880659bcea08905e6c57f358ac1.png)\n\nLet's describe the three possible types of object formed by the image of $\\mathcal P{:}$\n\n- Case where $T$ is non-singular:\n\nLet $\\mathbf T$ be the linear transformation with the following matrix representation:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe can easily check that $T$ is non-singular by computing its determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $\\mathcal P$ and then compute the product $TX{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is a triangle.\n\n![](images/14/145753e4f69dfa67bb45f59b7e9ed8bca789c589d4c5340ce2112363fa70cd7b.png)\n\n- Case where $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular and has at least one nonzero entry:\n\nLet $\\mathbf T$ be the linear transformation with the following matrix representation:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe can easily check that $T$ is singular by computing its determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3-6\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 4 & 10 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of  $\\mathbf{T}$ is a line segment.\n\n![](images/46/46d387dbf6678d324135dbbb3439688c0bdbf9f9567baef56f34aa4aa9ee22e3.png)\n\n- Case where $T$ is the zero matrix:\n\nLet $\\mathbf T$ be the linear transformation represented by the zero matrix:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nClearly, $T$ is singular. Now, computing the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are all $(0,0)$, the origin $O$ of the coordinate system. \n\n![](images/db/dbb9fce92da1f231a31e110f6cabb6faaf1bb43961a6c1de012c7c302c7af928.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107155 #edb/ref 1065 7702 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107155 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0bc95d9b-74ad-509c-8c28-e4e34606b376" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107155 #edb/ref 1067 "Image Types Formed by Linear Transformations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107156 #edb/ref 1064 "In general, for any linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ with associated $2 \\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-singular, i.e., $\\det(T) \\neq 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular, i.e., $\\det(T) = 0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107156 #edb/ref 1065 7708 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107156 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "593c9d77-3990-5aef-8f2f-1ef87dd41c59" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107156 #edb/ref 1067 "A Summary of Image Types" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184189 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050866 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184189 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184189 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184883 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184189 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184189 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262610 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184189 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186319170 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184189 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186319171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184189 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186320906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184189 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186320907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184189 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186320908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184189 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186320909 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184189 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351239 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184189 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351344 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184189 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351346 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184189 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351348 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184189 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186369338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184189 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416141 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184189 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing a Higher-Degree Taylor Polynomial Of a Given Function About a Given Point" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184189 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186184087 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184189 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "22d992a1-2ea8-5226-9971-188447f98ab6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184190 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184190 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184190 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262611 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184190 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184190 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184190 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186319172 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184190 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186320911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184190 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186320912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184190 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186320913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184190 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351240 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184190 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184190 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 175